# MODULE D: PROJECT PROJECTION & PLANNING

Create a Project Projections section visible to:

- Admin
- Management/CEO
- Executive Director

All three roles must be able to create, edit, and manage projections according to the same authorized planning capabilities, consistent with the existing role architecture.

## D1. Projection creation

A projection represents a proposed project before it is formally activated.

Capture relevant information such as:

- Projection title and unique reference
- Client or project owner name
- Client contact details, with appropriate privacy protections
- Proposed project name
- Project description and scope
- Site location
- Project category
- Expected start and completion dates
- Project specifications
- Project objectives and deliverables
- Estimated project price or contract value
- Projected material requirements
- Projected labour and artisan costs
- Projected logistics, transportation, and delivery costs
- Equipment and other applicable costs
- Contingency allowance
- Other relevant expenses
- Projected total cost
- Projected profit and margin, where the necessary inputs exist
- Planning notes, assumptions, risks, exclusions, and special conditions

Use a structured form with sections and autosave or explicit save functionality.

Allow authorized planners to add, edit, remove, and reorder relevant line items.

Do not invent financial values or silently insert default costs that could be mistaken for real estimates.

## D2. Projected material costing

Each projection must contain a dedicated Materials Estimate section connected to the central catalogue.

Authorized planners can select relevant materials and specify:

- Required quantity
- Unit
- Projected unit price
- Calculated line total
- Estimated supplier or source
- Expected procurement lead time
- Expected delivery date
- Notes and specifications

Calculate the projected materials subtotal and overall project cost accurately.

Allow materials to be added or removed from a projection independently of the master catalogue.

Changing a projected quantity or price must not change the master catalogue's standard price or alter another project's estimate.

Where actual prices later become available, preserve both projected and actual figures for comparison.

## D3. Labour, logistics, equipment, and other costs

Provide flexible line-item sections for:

- Labour and artisans
- Site supervision
- Technical inspection
- Transport and logistics
- Tools and equipment
- Production or fabrication where applicable
- Other project-specific costs
- Contingencies

Support quantity, unit, rate, line total, description, and notes where appropriate.

Avoid hard-coding assumptions that all projects have identical cost categories.

Calculate subtotals and the overall projected cost consistently.

## D4. Planning versions and change history

Every projection must have a clear status, for example:

Draft → Internal Review → Awaiting Inputs → Ready for Approval → Approved → Converted to Project.

Allow authorized planners to revise an ongoing projection at any stage permitted by its status.

Maintain a version or change history that records who changed the projection, when it changed, and the relevant before-and-after values.

Where a projection has already been approved, record subsequent revisions and whether renewed approval is required.

Do not erase the history of an approved projection.

# MODULE E: ROLE-BASED PLANNING CONTRIBUTIONS

Admin, Management/CEO, and Executive Director can invite selected roles to contribute information to a projection.

Examples include:

- Technical Inspection & QC: specifications, quality requirements, site conditions, and technical feasibility.
- Site Supervisor: site assessment, material quantities, labour needs, timelines, and execution constraints.
- Procurement & Logistics: supplier quotations, availability, lead times, delivery estimates, and logistics costs.
- Accounts: cost validation, budget review, payment assumptions, and financial observations.
- Artisan/Workforce: practical execution estimates, labour inputs, and task-specific requirements.

Use the application's actual role names and existing role-assignment mechanisms.

## E1. Invitation and instructions

For every invited role, the planner must specify:

- Invited role or authorized individual
- Scope of the requested contribution
- Clear instructions
- Questions to answer
- Required information or documents
- Expected deliverables
- Deadline
- Priority
- Supporting context or files where supported

Track invitation status, acknowledgement, submission, review, and completion.

## E2. Contributor workspace

Invited contributors should see only the projections and information they are authorized to access.

Provide a dedicated workspace showing:

- Projection summary
- Assigned request for input
- Planner's instructions
- Relevant context
- Submission form
- Notes and supporting evidence
- Deadline and status
- Submission history

Contributors may submit findings, estimates, observations, quotations, recommended quantities, risks, and supporting evidence.

**Contributors must not edit the master projection, its official material plan, its main financial totals, or its planning assumptions directly.**

Their findings must be stored as separate submissions linked to the projection and the relevant invitation.

After submission, authorized planners can review the contribution and choose to incorporate relevant information into the master projection.

A contributor's recommendation must never silently change the master projection.

Allow planners to accept, reject, request clarification, or mark a contribution as reviewed. Preserve the original submission and review history.

## E3. Access control

Apply least-privilege access. Inviting a role does not grant it unrestricted access to all projections or project financial information.

Ensure that invited contributors cannot access other projections unless explicitly authorized.

Validate access on the server/database layer through Supabase RLS and secure RPCs or existing authorized service patterns. Frontend visibility alone is not security.

# MODULE F: PROJECTION APPROVAL AND PDF GENERATION

## F1. Approval

Authorized planners must be able to review the completed projection and mark it ready for approval or approved according to the existing approval model.

Capture the approving user, date, relevant notes, and approved version.

Do not automatically activate a project simply because a PDF was generated.

## F2. Client-ready PDF

Generate a professional, printable, shareable PDF project proposal or summary.

It should be suitable for sharing through WhatsApp, email, and other channels with the project owner or client.

Include, where appropriate:

1. Davejoe Interiors branding and professional layout.
2. Proposal title and unique reference.
3. Date of preparation and validity period if specified.
4. Client or project owner details.
5. Project description and scope.
6. Deliverables and specifications.
7. Proposed timeline and milestones.
8. Materials schedule, including descriptions, quantities, units, projected prices, and subtotals where appropriate.
9. Labour, logistics, equipment, and other relevant cost breakdowns.
10. Overall projected cost or proposal price, with the basis of the figure clearly stated.
11. Relevant assumptions, exclusions, and terms.
12. Approval or acceptance section if required.
13. Appropriate contact details already stored in the application.

Use the existing branding and a polished document layout. Do not expose internal-only notes, confidential supplier information, private staff records, or internal profit calculations in the client-facing PDF unless an authorized user explicitly selects an appropriate internal report instead.

Support a client-facing PDF and, if practical, a separate internal planning/costing PDF.

Ensure currency formatting uses the appropriate project currency. Use Nigerian naira when that is the specified currency.

The PDF must be readable on mobile devices, with sensible page breaks, repeating table headers where appropriate, and no clipped content.

Allow an authorized user to preview the document before downloading or sharing it.

Do not claim to send a WhatsApp message automatically unless a supported integration is configured. At minimum, provide a downloadable PDF and a straightforward sharing workflow compatible with the device.

Store document metadata and the projection version it represents. If a projection changes, make it clear that an older PDF may no longer reflect the current version.

# MODULE G: CONVERT APPROVED PROJECTION INTO A PROJECT

After an authorized user approves a projection, provide an explicit action:

**Convert to Project**

The conversion must create or update the appropriate record in the existing Projects module using the approved projection.

Carry forward the relevant data:

- Project name and description
- Client information permitted for project records
- Site location
- Planned start and completion dates
- Scope and deliverables
- Approved material requirements
- Budget and cost breakdowns
- Technical specifications
- Approved assumptions and relevant notes
- Project planning reference and source projection
- Appropriate documents

Link the resulting project to its source projection.

Preserve the approved projection and its version history.

Avoid duplicate project creation if the conversion action is retried. Use a transaction or secure idempotent database operation to prevent duplicate projects and duplicate material requirements.

The new project must be registered in Supabase and visible in the existing Projects module.

Once converted, authorized roles can continue to edit the active project using the existing project-management workflows. Keep the original approved projection available as a historical baseline.

Do not automatically assign all nine roles. Provide the existing role assignment workflow so authorized users can assign relevant staff and responsibilities.

