# MODULE I: DATABASE, RLS, AND AUDITABILITY

Implement the features through the existing Supabase architecture.

Before designing migrations, map each requirement to existing tables and identify only the genuinely missing structures or fields.

Potential data concepts include:

- Project material requirements and allocations
- Material requests and request items
- Dispatches and dispatch line items
- Receipts and receipt discrepancies
- Artisan material allocations
- Material usage reports and usage line items
- Material remnant handovers and reconciliation
- Project projections and version history
- Projection line items and cost categories
- Projection invitations and contributor submissions
- Projection approval history
- Generated proposal documents

These are conceptual requirements, not instructions to create a separate table for every item. Reuse existing tables where suitable and preserve their established relationships.

Use proper foreign keys, indexes, timestamps, status constraints, quantity validation, and audit fields.

For material quantities, use appropriate numeric types and units. Do not assume all materials use whole-number quantities.

Use transactions or secure database functions for operations that affect multiple related records, including projection conversion, dispatch, receipt, allocation, and reconciliation.

Apply restrictive RLS policies for all new or modified tables.

- Only Admin, Management/CEO, and Executive Director can manage the master materials catalogue and the master projection.
- Site Supervisors can act within their assigned project scope.
- Technical personnel can act within their assigned projects and permitted technical responsibilities.
- Artisans can submit and view their own authorized material receipts and reports.
- Procurement & Logistics can perform authorized procurement and dispatch responsibilities.
- Accounts can access the financial information and contribution workflows authorized for their role.
- Invited contributors can submit their own assigned inputs without editing the master projection.

Use the project's actual role identifiers and helper functions after verifying them. Do not invent new role names or bypass existing permission checks.

Do not expose Supabase service-role credentials to the browser.

Record important actions in the existing audit log, including catalogue changes, material approvals, dispatches, receipts, discrepancy resolutions, allocations, usage submissions, projection changes, invitations, reviews, approvals, PDF generation, and project conversion.

Preserve historical records. Use controlled corrections and reversal transactions rather than silently overwriting completed material transactions.

# MODULE J: TESTING AND ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

Implement and test the following scenarios:

1. An authorized user creates a material in the catalogue before any project exists.
2. An unauthorized user cannot create or modify catalogue entries.
3. A planner selects only the materials needed for a particular project.
4. A project with no requirement for a particular material does not receive an automatic allocation for it.
5. A Site Supervisor sees materials actually assigned to their project.
6. A supervisor requests additional material, provides a reason, and specifies a required delivery date.
7. Admin, Management, and Executive Director can review and act on that request.
8. A request can be partially approved and partially fulfilled without losing its outstanding balance.
9. A dispatch records the issuing person, recipient, quantity, destination, and expected delivery.
10. The receiver records actual quantity and condition, including shortages or damage.
11. A technical inspector can record a material quality inspection.
12. An authorized supervisor or technical person allocates available materials to an artisan.
13. An artisan acknowledges receipt and submits a usage report.
14. A discrepancy between quantities is flagged instead of silently corrected.
15. Remaining materials can be handed over to an authorized recipient and reconciled.
16. An authorized planner creates a projection with materials, quantities, estimated prices, labour, logistics, and other costs.
17. An invited contributor can submit findings but cannot modify the master projection.
18. A planner can review a submission and incorporate the approved information manually.
19. Changes to the projection preserve the appropriate history.
20. An approved projection generates a client-facing PDF with correct totals and no internal-only information.
21. An authorized user converts the approved projection into an active project.
22. Retrying conversion does not create duplicate projects or material requirements.
23. All newly introduced permissions are enforced by Supabase, not only by frontend checks.
24. Existing authentication, dashboards, projects, approvals, attendance, notifications, and audit functionality continue to work.

Run the available typecheck, lint, tests, and production build.

Do not claim tests passed unless they were actually run and their results are available.

